  "description": "Verify speculative draft tokens against target model distribution probabilities using acceptance probability criteria.\n\nSpeculative decoding achieves 2x-3x speedup by drafting cheap tokens with a small model and verifying in parallel with the large model.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nP(\\text{accept}) = \\min\\left(1, \\frac{P_{\\text{target}}(x)}{P_{\\text{draft}}(x)}\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compare draft model probability against target model probability for each proposed token\n2. Accept draft token if acceptance ratio satisfies acceptance condition\n3. Halt on first rejected token and sample correction from residual distribution\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'accepted_tokens', 'num_accepted', and 'acceptance_rate'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Speculative Decoding and Token Verification\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Implement Speculative Decoding and Token Verification** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nSpeculative decoding achieves 2x-3x speedup by drafting cheap tokens with a small model and verifying in parallel with the large model. In this problem, the goal is to: verify speculative draft tokens against target model distribution probabilities using acceptance probability criteria.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{accept}) = \\min\\left(1, \\frac{P_{\\text{target}}(x)}{P_{\\text{draft}}(x)}\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compare draft model probability against target model probability for each proposed token\n2. **Phase 2**: Accept draft token if acceptance ratio satisfies acceptance condition\n3. **Phase 3**: Halt on first rejected token and sample correction from residual distribution\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: draft_tokens = [101, 102, 103], draft_probs = [0.9, 0.8, 0.7], target_probs = [0.85, 0.75, 0.2]\n\nOutput: {\"accepted_tokens\": [101, 102], \"num_accepted\": 2, \"acceptance_rate\": 0.6667}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated inference_speculative_decoding_verification under standard test conditions."
